-- Q4647: Specific Areas and Transactions [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Require the entity to eliminate the related party transaction entirely","Obtain sufficient appropriate evidence about proper accounting and disclosure","Ignore the transaction if it falls below planning materiality","Report the transaction directly to the SEC for investigation"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 550, the auditor should obtain sufficient appropriate audit evidence to determine whether related party transactions have been properly identified, accounted for, and disclosed per the applicable framework. This includes understanding the nature and business purpose of the transactions.
Wrong (A): The auditor cannot require elimination of a legitimate transaction; the role is to evaluate accounting and disclosure.
Wrong (C): Related party transactions require evaluation regardless of dollar amount due to inherent fraud risk.
Wrong (D): There is no automatic requirement to report related party transactions to the SEC.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4647;

-- Q4651: Specific Areas and Transactions [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Issue an unmodified opinion on the financial statements","Issue an adverse opinion due to inappropriate going concern basis","Issue a qualified opinion with an explanatory paragraph added","Issue a disclaimer of opinion on the financial statements"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 570, when the entity cannot continue as a going concern but the financial statements are prepared on a going concern basis, that basis is inappropriate. The resulting material misstatement requires the auditor to express an adverse opinion.
Wrong (A): An unmodified opinion is inappropriate because the financial statements are materially misstated due to the wrong basis.
Wrong (C): A qualified opinion understates the severity; the inappropriate basis pervasively affects the statements.
Wrong (D): A disclaimer addresses insufficient evidence, not a known material misstatement.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4651;

-- Q4653: Specific Areas and Transactions [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The transaction may not reflect economic reality and could be used to manipulate results","All related party transactions are presumed to be fraudulent in nature","Related parties are prohibited from entering into transactions with each other","The transaction will automatically result in a material misstatement of financials"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 550, related party transactions at non-arm''s-length terms are concerning because they may not reflect economic substance and could be structured to manipulate financial results. The auditor must evaluate whether such transactions are properly disclosed and accounted for.
Wrong (B): Related party transactions are not presumed fraudulent; they are legal but require heightened scrutiny.
Wrong (C): GAAP does not prohibit related party transactions; it requires appropriate disclosure.
Wrong (D): Non-arm''s-length terms do not automatically create a material misstatement; the auditor must assess the impact.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4653;

-- Q4654: Specific Areas and Transactions [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Request management to extend its assessment to cover at least twelve months","Accept the shorter assessment period without further comment or action","Issue a disclaimer of opinion due to the shortened evaluation period","Modify the evaluation period to six months from the balance sheet date"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 570 and ASC 205-40, management must evaluate whether substantial doubt exists about going concern ability for at least one year after the financial statements are issued. If the assessment covers less than this period, the auditor should request an extension.
Wrong (B): Accepting a shorter period fails to comply with the required twelve-month evaluation window.
Wrong (C): A shortened assessment alone does not warrant a disclaimer; the auditor should first request an extension.
Wrong (D): The auditor cannot unilaterally modify the evaluation period; management must perform the assessment.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4654;

-- Q4655: Specific Areas and Transactions [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Evaluate the appropriateness of methods, assumptions, and data used by management","Focus only on whether the mathematical calculations are arithmetically correct","Accept all estimates since they inherently involve some degree of uncertainty","Only test estimates that exceed a predetermined dollar threshold amount"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 540, the auditor must evaluate whether management''s methods are appropriate, whether significant assumptions are reasonable and supported by evidence, and whether the underlying data is relevant, reliable, and complete. The auditor should also consider management bias.
Wrong (B): Mathematical accuracy alone is insufficient; the auditor must also assess assumptions and methodology.
Wrong (C): Uncertainty does not excuse the auditor from evaluating reasonableness; estimates still require audit evidence.
Wrong (D): All material estimates require evaluation regardless of whether they exceed a specific threshold.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4655;

-- Q4657: Specific Areas and Transactions [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Reviewing large or unusual transactions near year-end for undisclosed relationships","Performing a physical observation of warehouse inventory on the count date","Testing depreciation calculations for fixed assets on the balance sheet","Confirming year-end bank balances with third-party financial institutions"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 550, reviewing large or unusual transactions — particularly those near year-end, with unusual terms, or lacking apparent business rationale — is an effective procedure to identify previously undisclosed related parties. Such transactions may signal hidden management relationships.
Wrong (B): Physical inventory observation tests existence and condition of inventory, not related party relationships.
Wrong (C): Testing depreciation calculations addresses valuation of fixed assets, not related party identification.
Wrong (D): Bank confirmations verify cash balances and debt, not vendor or management relationships.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4657;

-- Q4658: Specific Areas and Transactions [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Replace the opinion paragraph entirely with going concern language","Follow the opinion paragraph and reference substantial doubt about operations","Be placed before the opinion paragraph and reference the specific disclosure note","Omit any reference to specific notes in the financial statement disclosures"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 570, for non-issuers the going concern emphasis-of-matter paragraph is placed in a separate section that includes the heading "Going Concern" and references the specific financial statement note discussing the uncertainty. It should appear with clear identification of the matter.
Wrong (A): The emphasis-of-matter paragraph supplements the opinion paragraph; it does not replace it.
Wrong (B): The paragraph must specifically reference the note disclosure, not merely mention "substantial doubt" generically.
Wrong (D): The paragraph must reference the specific note in the financial statements that discusses the going concern matter.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4658;

-- Q4659: Specific Areas and Transactions [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["They are always illegal regardless of the terms or disclosures involved","They always result in tax penalties imposed by regulatory authorities","They may not be at arm''s length and may serve financial reporting objectives","They require footnote disclosure only, with no effect on statement amounts"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 550, the primary audit risk with related party transactions is that they may not be conducted at arm''s length. The terms may not reflect fair market conditions, creating risk that financial statements do not represent underlying economics and that transactions were structured for reporting purposes.
Wrong (A): Related party transactions are not inherently illegal; they are permitted but require proper disclosure.
Wrong (B): Related party transactions do not automatically trigger tax penalties; tax treatment depends on the specific facts.
Wrong (D): Related party transactions may affect both financial statement amounts and footnote disclosures, not just footnotes.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4659;

-- Q4660: Specific Areas and Transactions [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Level 3 inputs are observable and can be independently verified by auditors","Level 3 measurements are typically immaterial to the overall financial statements","Level 3 inputs are unobservable and rely heavily on management''s own assumptions","Level 3 is the most commonly used category across fair value measurements"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 540 and ASC 820, Level 3 fair value measurements use significant unobservable inputs based on management''s own assumptions and models rather than observable market data. This creates high estimation uncertainty, difficulty in independent verification, and susceptibility to management bias.
Wrong (A): Level 3 inputs are unobservable by definition; observable inputs characterize Level 1 and Level 2.
Wrong (B): Level 3 measurements can be highly material, particularly for complex financial instruments and illiquid assets.
Wrong (D): Level 3 is typically the least common category; most measurements use observable Level 1 or Level 2 inputs.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4660;

-- Q4662: Specific Areas and Transactions [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Transactions that follow standard industry practices and normal terms","Transactions with clear business rationale and arm''s-length pricing","Transactions lacking business substance or involving undisclosed related parties","Transactions that involve only immaterial amounts below planning materiality"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 240 and AU-C 550, the auditor should be alert for significant unusual transactions that lack business substance, involve previously unidentified related parties, or have been processed outside normal channels. These characteristics are potential indicators of fraud or financial statement manipulation.
Wrong (A): Standard industry transactions with normal terms present lower fraud risk and are not the primary concern.
Wrong (B): Transactions with clear rationale and arm''s-length pricing are less likely to indicate manipulation.
Wrong (D): Immaterial transactions are generally lower risk; the auditor focuses on significant unusual transactions.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4662;

-- Q4663: Specific Areas and Transactions [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Is expected in normal operations and does not affect the audit approach","Only needs documentation if the bias exceeds ten percent of the estimate","Automatically requires the auditor to issue an adverse opinion on the statements","Should be evaluated for cumulative effect to determine if misstatement is material"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 540, management bias in estimates does not automatically constitute a material misstatement. However, the auditor must evaluate the cumulative effect of biased assumptions across all estimates. If the cumulative effect is material, or if the bias suggests possible intent to mislead, appropriate action is required.
Wrong (A): Consistent bias is not expected and does affect the audit; it may indicate a fraud risk factor.
Wrong (B): There is no ten-percent threshold for documenting bias; all identified bias requires evaluation.
Wrong (C): Bias does not automatically require an adverse opinion; the auditor must first assess materiality and pervasiveness.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4663;

-- Q4667: Modifications to the Auditor Report [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The financial statements contain only immaterial misstatements overall","Insufficient evidence exists and the possible effects are material and pervasive","The auditor discovers fraud during the course of the audit engagement","Management cooperates fully but the auditor disagrees with accounting treatment"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 705, a disclaimer of opinion is expressed when the auditor cannot obtain sufficient appropriate audit evidence on which to base an opinion, and the possible effects of undetected misstatements could be both material and pervasive. This occurs when scope limitations are so severe the auditor cannot form an opinion.
Wrong (A): If misstatements are only immaterial, an unmodified opinion is appropriate.
Wrong (C): Discovery of fraud does not automatically require a disclaimer; the response depends on materiality and pervasiveness.
Wrong (D): Disagreement with accounting treatment is a departure issue leading to a qualified or adverse opinion, not a disclaimer.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4667;

-- Q4668: Modifications to the Auditor Report [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Modify the auditor''s opinion on the financial statements as presented","Highlight a properly disclosed matter fundamental to users'' understanding","Replace the basis for opinion paragraph with alternative language","Limit the auditor''s liability for matters disclosed in the statements"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 706, an emphasis-of-matter paragraph draws attention to a matter appropriately presented or disclosed in the financial statements that is of such importance it is fundamental to users'' understanding. Examples include going concern uncertainties and significant related party transactions. It does not modify the opinion.
Wrong (A): An emphasis-of-matter paragraph does not modify the audit opinion; the opinion remains unmodified.
Wrong (C): The emphasis-of-matter paragraph is a separate section; it does not replace the basis for opinion paragraph.
Wrong (D): The purpose is user communication, not liability limitation; it has no legal effect on auditor responsibility.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4668;

-- Q4669: Modifications to the Auditor Report [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Always modifies the audit opinion expressed on the financial statements","Addresses matters not in the financial statements but relevant to the audit","Is used exclusively for going concern issues identified during the engagement","Replaces the opinion paragraph in the auditor''s standard report format"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 706, an other-matter paragraph addresses matters not presented or disclosed in the financial statements but relevant to users'' understanding of the audit, the auditor''s responsibilities, or the auditor''s report. Examples include references to a prior-period auditor or restrictions on report distribution.
Wrong (A): Neither an emphasis-of-matter nor an other-matter paragraph modifies the audit opinion.
Wrong (C): Going concern issues are addressed through emphasis-of-matter paragraphs, not other-matter paragraphs.
Wrong (D): The other-matter paragraph supplements the report; it does not replace the opinion paragraph.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4669;

-- Q4671: Modifications to the Auditor Report [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Only the standard unmodified opinion language without additional sections","A basis for qualified opinion paragraph describing the misstatement and effects","A reference to the client''s external legal counsel''s engagement letter","An emphasis-of-matter paragraph used in place of a basis for opinion section"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 705, a qualified opinion for a framework departure requires a basis for qualified opinion paragraph. This paragraph describes the nature of the misstatement, quantifies its effect if practicable, and explains why the departure is material. The opinion paragraph then includes "except for" language.
Wrong (A): Standard unmodified language omits the required basis paragraph and "except for" wording.
Wrong (C): Legal counsel letters are not referenced in the basis for qualified opinion paragraph.
Wrong (D): An emphasis-of-matter paragraph highlights properly disclosed matters; it cannot substitute for the basis for modification.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4671;

-- Q4672: Modifications to the Auditor Report [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["An unmodified opinion since alternative procedures were attempted","An adverse opinion because the inventory records are unreliable","A disclaimer of opinion due to pervasive scope limitation effects","A qualified opinion due to a material but not pervasive scope limitation"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 705, when the auditor cannot obtain sufficient appropriate evidence due to a scope limitation and the possible effects are material but not pervasive, a qualified opinion is appropriate. The basis for qualified opinion paragraph should describe the scope limitation and its potential effects.
Wrong (A): An unmodified opinion requires sufficient evidence; attempting alternatives that fail does not satisfy this requirement.
Wrong (B): An adverse opinion addresses known material misstatements, not insufficient evidence from scope limitations.
Wrong (C): A disclaimer is warranted only when the possible effects are both material and pervasive, not merely material.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4672;

-- Q4673: Modifications to the Auditor Report [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A scope limitation prevents obtaining evidence for multiple significant accounts","The entity changed its depreciation method from straight-line to accelerated","The entity adopted a new standard that changed revenue presentation format","The auditor disagrees with management''s estimate of the bad debt allowance"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 705, a disclaimer of opinion is appropriate when scope limitations are so severe and pervasive that the auditor cannot obtain sufficient evidence to form an opinion. Inability to audit multiple significant accounts representing a substantial portion of the financial statements meets both the material and pervasive thresholds.
Wrong (B): A change in depreciation method is an accounting change requiring evaluation, not a scope limitation issue.
Wrong (C): Adopting a new standard affects presentation but does not prevent the auditor from obtaining evidence.
Wrong (D): Disagreement with an estimate is a departure issue that leads to a qualified or adverse opinion, not a disclaimer.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4673;

-- Q4674: Modifications to the Auditor Report [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Except for the effects of the matter described, the statements present fairly","In our opinion, the financial statements present fairly in all material respects","We do not express an opinion on the accompanying financial statements","In our opinion, the financial statements do not present fairly overall"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 705, a qualified opinion uses "except for" language: "In our opinion, except for the effects of the matter described in the Basis for Qualified Opinion section, the financial statements present fairly, in all material respects." This communicates that the qualification is limited to a specific identified matter.
Wrong (B): This is unmodified opinion language that does not acknowledge the known departure from GAAP.
Wrong (C): This is disclaimer language used when the auditor cannot obtain sufficient evidence, not for a known departure.
Wrong (D): This is adverse opinion language indicating the statements are materially misstated overall, which overstates the issue.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4674;

-- Q4676: Modifications to the Auditor Report [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The extent to which the misstatement affects or could affect the statements overall","Whether the misstatement is large in dollar amount relative to total assets","Whether management agrees with the auditor''s assessment of the misstatement","The number of audit procedures the engagement team performed during fieldwork"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 705, pervasiveness describes how widely misstatements spread across the financial statements. A misstatement is pervasive if it is not confined to specific elements, represents a substantial proportion of the financial statements, or fundamentally undermines the usefulness of disclosures. This determines whether a qualified or adverse opinion is appropriate.
Wrong (B): Dollar magnitude alone does not determine pervasiveness; a large but isolated misstatement may be material but not pervasive.
Wrong (C): Management''s agreement or disagreement is irrelevant to the auditor''s assessment of pervasiveness.
Wrong (D): The number of procedures performed relates to audit scope, not to the pervasiveness of identified misstatements.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4676;

-- Q4678: Modifications to the Auditor Report [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["An unmodified opinion because management was not at fault for the limitation","An adverse opinion because the accounting records have been permanently destroyed","A qualified opinion or disclaimer depending on materiality and pervasiveness","No effect on the auditor''s report since circumstances were beyond control"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 705, a scope limitation — regardless of whether imposed by management or by circumstances — requires the auditor to evaluate the potential effects on the financial statements. If material but not pervasive, a qualified opinion is appropriate. If material and pervasive, a disclaimer is warranted.
Wrong (A): The cause of the limitation is irrelevant; the auditor must still evaluate the effect of missing evidence.
Wrong (B): An adverse opinion addresses known material misstatements, not inability to obtain evidence.
Wrong (D): Any scope limitation that prevents obtaining sufficient evidence affects the auditor''s report.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4678;

-- Q4680: Modifications to the Auditor Report [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Issue an unmodified opinion because contingent liabilities are inherently uncertain","Issue a disclaimer of opinion due to the missing disclosure information","Issue a qualified opinion and include the omitted disclosure to extent practicable","Issue an unmodified opinion with an emphasis-of-matter paragraph about the item"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 705, when required disclosures are omitted and the omission is material but not pervasive, the auditor should issue a qualified opinion. The basis for qualified opinion paragraph should describe the omission and, to the extent practicable, include the missing information so users understand the impact.
Wrong (A): Uncertainty does not excuse omission of required disclosures; the departure still warrants a modified opinion.
Wrong (B): A disclaimer addresses insufficient evidence, not a known disclosure omission by management.
Wrong (D): An emphasis-of-matter paragraph is for properly disclosed matters; omitted disclosures require opinion modification.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4680;

-- Q4681: Modifications to the Auditor Report [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Describe all the specific audit procedures the engagement team performed","Describe management''s responsibilities for the financial statements presented","List all misstatements found during the course of the audit engagement","Provide the foundation for the opinion and, if modified, the reasons why"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 700, the basis for opinion section states the audit was conducted in accordance with GAAS, references the auditor''s independence and ethical responsibilities, and confirms sufficient appropriate evidence was obtained. For modified opinions, it specifically describes the reasons for the modification.
Wrong (A): The basis section references GAAS compliance generally but does not list specific audit procedures performed.
Wrong (B): Management''s responsibilities are described in a separate section of the auditor''s report.
Wrong (C): The section does not list all misstatements; it describes only the basis for the opinion or modification.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4681;

-- Q4682: Modifications to the Auditor Report [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["An adverse opinion on all of the financial statements taken as a whole","An unmodified opinion on all of the financial statements taken as a whole","A disclaimer of opinion on all of the financial statements taken as a whole","A qualified opinion on the affected statement and unmodified on the others"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 705, when a material misstatement affects only one financial statement and does not pervasively affect the others, the auditor may issue a qualified opinion on the affected statement while issuing an unmodified opinion on the unaffected statements. This approach communicates the specific nature of the issue.
Wrong (A): An adverse opinion on all statements overstates the issue when only one statement is affected.
Wrong (B): An unmodified opinion ignores the identified material misstatement on the affected statement.
Wrong (C): A disclaimer addresses insufficient evidence, not a known misstatement confined to one statement.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4682;

-- Q4683: Modifications to the Auditor Report [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Going concern uncertainty identified by the auditor during the engagement","A change in accounting principle adopted by management in the current period","A significant subsequent event that has been appropriately disclosed in notes","A known departure from the applicable financial reporting framework used"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 706, an emphasis-of-matter paragraph highlights matters appropriately presented or disclosed in the financial statements. Going concern, changes in accounting principles, and disclosed subsequent events qualify. A known departure from the framework is not appropriate for emphasis-of-matter — it requires a qualified or adverse opinion instead.
Wrong (A): Going concern uncertainty is a classic use of the emphasis-of-matter paragraph per AU-C 570.
Wrong (B): A change in accounting principle is specifically listed as an appropriate emphasis-of-matter topic.
Wrong (C): A significant subsequent event properly disclosed may warrant an emphasis-of-matter paragraph.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4683;

-- Q4684: Modifications to the Auditor Report [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Include the standard auditor responsibility language and confirm evidence obtained","Include an emphasis-of-matter paragraph highlighting the scope limitation issue","Be identical to an unmodified report except for the modified opinion paragraph","Omit the reasonable assurance statement and describe the reasons for disclaimer"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 705, when disclaiming an opinion, the report must not include standard statements about obtaining reasonable assurance or that sufficient appropriate evidence was obtained. The basis for disclaimer paragraph should describe why the auditor could not form an opinion, and the report states that no opinion is expressed.
Wrong (A): Including the standard responsibility language would contradict the disclaimer; the auditor cannot claim evidence was obtained.
Wrong (B): An emphasis-of-matter paragraph is used with unmodified opinions, not disclaimers due to scope limitations.
Wrong (C): A disclaimer report differs materially from an unmodified report in multiple sections, not just the opinion.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4684;

-- Q4685: Other Information and Supplementary Information [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The audited financial statements and their related notes and disclosures","Financial and non-financial information in a document alongside audited statements","Only the management discussion and analysis section of the annual report","Supplementary schedules that are audited together with the financial statements"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 720, other information refers to financial and non-financial information (other than the audited financial statements and the auditor''s report) included in a document containing audited financial statements. Examples include CEO letters, statistical summaries, and management discussion.
Wrong (A): The audited financial statements and notes are the subject of the audit opinion, not "other information."
Wrong (C): MD&A is one example of other information, but the definition is broader and includes all non-audited content.
Wrong (D): Supplementary schedules subjected to audit procedures are addressed under AU-C 725, not AU-C 720.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4685;

-- Q4686: Other Information and Supplementary Information [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Audit the other information to the same extent as the financial statements","Read it and consider whether it is materially inconsistent with audited statements","Ignore the other information entirely because it is not subject to audit","Provide a separate audit opinion on the other information in the report"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 720, the auditor must read the other information and consider whether it is materially inconsistent with the audited financial statements or the auditor''s knowledge obtained during the audit. The auditor remains alert to material inconsistencies or material misstatements of fact.
Wrong (A): The auditor does not audit other information; the responsibility is limited to reading and considering consistency.
Wrong (C): Ignoring other information violates AU-C 720; the auditor has a responsibility to read and evaluate it.
Wrong (D): The auditor does not express a separate opinion on other information under AU-C 720.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4686;

-- Q4687: Other Information and Supplementary Information [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Is required by the framework to accompany the basic financial statements presented","Is always audited to the same extent as the basic financial statement amounts","Is optional and can be omitted without any consequence to the audit report","Is prepared by the auditor rather than by management or those charged with governance"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 730, required supplementary information (RSI) is information that a designated accounting standards setter such as GASB or FASB requires to accompany the basic financial statements. While RSI is not part of the basic statements, its omission would be a departure from prescribed guidelines.
Wrong (B): RSI is not audited; the auditor applies limited procedures such as inquiries and comparison to the statements.
Wrong (C): RSI is required by the standards setter; omission triggers reporting obligations for the auditor.
Wrong (D): RSI is prepared by management, not the auditor; the auditor''s role is to apply limited procedures.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4687;

-- Q4688: Other Information and Supplementary Information [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Ignore the inconsistency because the other information is not subject to audit","Determine whether the statements or other information needs revision with management","Immediately issue a qualified opinion on the audited financial statements","Report the inconsistency only to the SEC for further regulatory investigation"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 720, when a material inconsistency is identified between the other information and the audited financial statements, the auditor should determine which needs revision and discuss the matter with management. If the financial statements need revision, the audit opinion may be affected.
Wrong (A): Ignoring a material inconsistency violates AU-C 720, even though the other information is not audited.
Wrong (C): A material inconsistency in other information does not automatically require a qualified opinion on the financial statements.
Wrong (D): There is no automatic requirement to report to the SEC; the auditor first works with management to resolve the matter.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4688;

-- Q4689: Other Information and Supplementary Information [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Required supplementary information mandated by the standards setter","Other information as defined under the AU-C 720 reporting standard","Supplementary information accompanying the audited financial statements","Additional voluntary disclosures not subject to any audit procedures"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 725, supplementary information accompanying the audited financial statements refers to information presented outside the basic financial statements that is not required by the framework but that management chooses to include. The auditor may report on it in relation to the financial statements as a whole.
Wrong (A): Required supplementary information (RSI) under AU-C 730 is mandated by a standards setter, unlike voluntary supplementary information.
Wrong (B): Other information under AU-C 720 is in documents containing audited statements but is not presented as supplementary to them.
Wrong (D): Supplementary information under AU-C 725 may be subjected to audit procedures when the auditor is engaged to report on it.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4689;
